{% lede %}
As a graduate student in Jennifer Lewis's research group in the Materials Science Department at the University of Illinois, I directed, filmed and edited this video highlighting our group's work, and created its motion graphics. Chemical & Engineering News published it, and it has about 250,000 views.
{% /lede %}

The paper behind it, "Pen-on-Paper Flexible Electronics," described a silver ink that can be loaded into a pen to draw working circuits on paper. The video shows the whole idea in a few minutes: graduate student Analisa Russo explains the work, the ink is made on a clean white set, and a pen writes a word that conducts electricity.

## Graphics

The key moment pairs live footage with a graph: as the pen writes "conductive," a multimeter reads the line's resistance beside a curve of resistance falling over time. On-screen labels, like the playback speed and the paper's citation, keep the viewer oriented.

Click any image to watch. A photograph of the same pen-on-paper work is on the [Journal Cover and Scientific Figures](/work/journal-cover-and-scientific-figures/) page.
